# Implementation Plan — Configurable Moderator Role & Granular Permission System

Implement the **Moderator** role in Qualiva as a single base operational role whose access, navigation, available actions, and workspace are governed entirely by configurable module permissions assigned by an Administrator.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Single Base Role Principle:** No role fragmentation will take place. The database role will always be `MODERATOR`. Capabilities (e.g. Course Review, Enrollment Approval, User Auditing) are determined entirely by permission flags.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Registration Lifecycle:** When a user registers selecting the `MODERATOR` role, their account is initialized in `PENDING_APPROVAL` status. They cannot log in or perform actions until an Administrator reviews them, sets their specific permissions via a checklist modal, and activates their account.

---

## Proposed Architectural Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor User as New User
    actor Admin as System Administrator
    participant Client as Qualiva Frontend
    participant Server as Express API
    participant DB as Neon PostgreSQL

    User->>Client: Registers with Role = "Moderator"
    Client->>Server: POST /auth/register (status = PENDING_APPROVAL)
    Server->>DB: Create User (role=MODERATOR, status=PENDING_APPROVAL, permissions=null)
    Server-->>Admin: Real-time Alert (New Moderator Role Request)

    Admin->>Client: Opens Approvals Hub / User Profile
    Admin->>Client: Clicks "Approve as Moderator"
    Client->>Admin: Opens "Moderator Permissions Checklist" Modal
    Admin->>Client: Ticks authorized modules & actions (e.g., Courses: View+Manage, Enrollments: Approve)
    Admin->>Client: Clicks "Save Permissions & Activate Account"

    Client->>Server: PUT /api/v1/admin/users/:id/moderator-permissions
    Server->>DB: Update User (status=ACTIVE, moderatorPermissions=JSON)
    Server->>DB: Log to AuditLog (previous vs new permissions, admin actor)

    Note over User,Client: Moderator signs in
    User->>Client: Signs in to Qualiva
    Client->>Client: Navigation & Dashboard adapt to ticked permissions only
    Client->>Server: Calls API endpoints
    Server->>Server: `requireModeratorPermission(module, action)` enforces backend boundaries
```

